28. History stack and replace
browser maintain the history of navigation inside the history stack so that the back and forward buttons to work.
navigate("/host", { replace: true }) - in this example say we were on /login route and after login we redirect user to /host route then without using the { replace: true }
the history stack can look like below(user lands on the home page, then go to about page , the try to go to host page , then redirected to login page , after successful login, redirected to /host page)
/ -> /about -> /host -> /login -> /host
with replace, the history stack will look like(we do this on successful login, istead of just redirecting user to the /host page , we also replace the current route /login from the history). In this way, when user clicks on back button they are not /login page again 
/ -> /about -> /host -> /host
now we see the 2 /host route in histroy
fixing above
<Navigate 
                to="/login" 
                state={{message: "You must log in first"}} 
                replace
            />)

the above code replaces the protected route from the history

